==German==

===Etymology===
{{PIE root|de|dewk}}
From {{inh|de|gmh|ziehen}}, from {{inh|de|goh|ziohan}}, from {{inh|de|gem-pro|*teuhaną}}, from {{inh|de|ine-pro|*dewk-||to pull, lead}}. Cognate with {{cog|nds|tehn}}, {{cog|got|𐍄𐌹𐌿𐌷𐌰𐌽}}, {{cog|dum|tīen}}, {{cog|ang|tēon}}, {{cog|non|toga}}, {{cog|fy|tsjen}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈtsiːən/|/ˈtsiːn/|lang=de}}
* {{audio|De-ziehen.ogg|Audio|lang=de}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb-strong|class=2|zieht|zog|gezogen|past subjunctive=zöge|auxiliary=both}}

# {{lb|de|transitive|or|intransitive|auxiliary: “haben”}} to [[pull]] (e.g., a door handle); to [[drag]]
# {{lb|de|transitive|auxiliary: “haben”}} to [[draw]] (e.g. a weapon); to [[extract]]; to [[puff]]
# {{lb|de|transitive|auxiliary: “haben”}} to [[draw]] (a conclusion, lesson, etc.)
#* '''2010''', ''{{w|Der Spiegel}}'', issue [http://www.spiegel.de/spiegel/print/index-2010-25.html 25/2010], page 77:
#*: {{quote|en|Es gilt deshalb, die richtigen Lehren aus der Krise zu '''ziehen''', aus den Fehlern der Vergangenheit zu lernen, um die Zukunft zu sichern.|Therefore it is necessary to draw the right lessons from the crisis, to learn from the mistakes of the past for securing the future.}}
# {{lb|de|impersonal|intransitive}} to [[be]] [[drafty]]
#: {{uxi|de|Es '''zieht'''.|It’s drafty.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|auxiliary: “sein”}} to [[move]]; to [[migrate]]
#: {{ux|de|Ich '''ziehe''' nach Hamburg, aber mein Bruder '''zieht''' in eine andere Stadt.|I'm '''moving''' to Hamburg, but my brother is '''moving''' to another city.}}
# {{lb|de|intransitive|auxiliary: “sein”}} to [[roam]]; to [[head]]
# {{lb|de|reflexive|auxiliary: “haben”}} to [[stretch]]; to [[warp]]

